---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';

// The accessibility page. Same shape as /ai and /privacy: versioned, dated, and
// honest about its own limits rather than claiming a conformance level.
//
// EVERYTHING STATED HERE WAS MEASURED ON 2026-08-22 against the built output of
// both sites, not asserted. The audit covered 27 pages on probablyfine.dev and
// 9 on maxfieldallison.com:
//   - alt text, <html lang>, <main>, skip links, h1 counts, heading-level order
//   - WCAG relative-luminance contrast for every palette token on both the base
//     and surface backgrounds
//   - prefers-reduced-motion and :focus-visible handling in global.css
//
// The audit found two contrast problems and BOTH WERE FIXED before this page
// shipped, which is why it lists no outstanding failures: --color-code-comment
// went #6e7a87 -> #7e8996 (4.42 -> 5.45 on base), and the small print dropped
// its 80% opacity (4.56 -> 6.58). The worst ratio in the palette is now 5.11.
//
// If you change a palette token, re-run the numbers and update this page in the
// same commit. Do not raise the claimed level without an audit that supports it,
// and if something regresses, say so here rather than quietly dropping the line.
//
// The arcade and the ZERO/DAY playtest are deliberately not mentioned. Both
// posts that link them are draft: true and excluded from the build, so nothing
// reachable points at them. WHEN THOSE POSTS GO LIVE, AUDIT THOSE PAGES AND
// REVISIT THIS ONE. Tracked in probablyfine-site#5.
const version = 'v1.0';
const updated = '2026-08-22';

const log = [
  {
    version: 'v1.0',
    date: '2026-08-22',
    body: 'First version, written from an audit of the built output of both sites rather than from intent. The audit turned up two contrast problems, in code comments and in the footer small print, and both were fixed before this page went up.',
  },
];
---

<Layout title="Accessibility · ProbablyFine" description="What I've actually checked, and what I haven't tested. Measured against the built sites, not claimed.">
  <section class="max-w-[var(--container-shell)] mx-auto px-6 pt-16 pb-8">
    <p class="font-mono text-sm text-[var(--color-accent)]">$ cat ./accessibility</p>
    <h1 class="text-3xl font-bold mt-3">Accessibility</h1>
    <p class="text-[var(--color-ink-dim)] mt-3 leading-relaxed">
      I build both of my sites against WCAG 2.2 AA. Everything I can measure currently meets it.
      I'm still not going to write "fully conformant" at the top of this page, because I haven't
      sat down with a screen reader yet, and that's the part no amount of measuring covers.
    </p>
    <p class="font-mono text-xs text-[var(--color-ink-dim)] mt-4">{version} · updated {updated}</p>
  </section>

  <section class="max-w-[var(--container-shell)] mx-auto px-6 pb-8">
    <div class="py-5 border-t border-[var(--color-line)]">
      <h2 class="font-mono text-sm text-[var(--color-accent)]"># what I checked</h2>
      <p class="text-[var(--color-ink-dim)] mt-2 leading-relaxed">
        Every page these sites link to, 36 of them. Each one declares its language, has a single
        h1, uses a real <span class="font-mono text-sm">&lt;main&gt;</span> landmark, and offers a
        skip link to it. No heading level is skipped anywhere. Every image has alt text.
      </p>
      <p class="text-[var(--color-ink-dim)] mt-3 leading-relaxed">
        Keyboard focus is visible on purpose rather than by browser default, and if your system
        asks for reduced motion the page transitions stop. Things you click are real buttons and
        links, so they work without a mouse.
      </p>
      <p class="text-[var(--color-ink-dim)] mt-3 leading-relaxed">
        I checked every colour in the palette against both backgrounds it gets used on. The
        standard asks for 4.5 to 1 on normal text. The weakest thing on the site is 5.1 to 1, body
        text is 16 to 1, and the dimmed text you're reading now is 6.6 to 1.
      </p>
      <p class="text-[var(--color-ink-dim)] mt-3 leading-relaxed">
        Two of those failed when I first measured them. Comments in code blocks sat at 4.42, and
        the small print in the footer scraped past at 4.56 on 11 pixel text. I changed both rather
        than list them here, which is the version of this page I'd rather write.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="py-5 border-t border-[var(--color-line)]">
      <h2 class="font-mono text-sm text-[var(--color-accent)]"># what I haven't checked</h2>
      <p class="text-[var(--color-ink-dim)] mt-2 leading-relaxed">
        A real screen reader. Everything above comes from measuring the markup and the colours,
        which catches the mechanical problems and tells you nothing about whether the thing is
        pleasant to listen to. Until I've done that, treat the AA target as a direction rather
        than a result.
      </p>
      <p class="text-[var(--color-ink-dim)] mt-3 leading-relaxed">
        The comment box is someone else's code running on my page. I picked it and I'm responsible
        for it being there, but I haven't audited it and I can't fix its internals. If it gets in
        your way, tell me, because that's the sort of thing that decides whether it stays.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="py-5 border-t border-[var(--color-line)]">
      <h2 class="font-mono text-sm text-[var(--color-accent)]"># if something blocks you</h2>
      <p class="text-[var(--color-ink-dim)] mt-2 leading-relaxed">
        Email me at{' '}
        <a href="mailto:hello@maxfieldallison.com" data-umami-event="email-click" class="hover:text-[var(--color-ink)]">hello@maxfieldallison.com</a>
        {' '}and tell me what you were trying to do. You don't need to know the terminology or
        which rule it breaks. "I couldn't get to the thing" is a complete bug report.
      </p>
      <p class="text-[var(--color-ink-dim)] mt-3 leading-relaxed">
        This is a personal site, so it's me reading that mail and me fixing it. No ticket number,
        but also nobody to route around.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="py-5 border-t border-[var(--color-line)]">
      <h2 class="font-mono text-sm text-[var(--color-accent)]"># log</h2>
      {
        log.map((e) => (
          <p class="text-[var(--color-ink-dim)] mt-2 leading-relaxed">
            <span class="font-mono text-sm text-[var(--color-ink)]">{e.version} · {e.date}</span> · {e.body}
          </p>
        ))
      }
      <p class="text-[var(--color-ink-dim)] mt-3 leading-relaxed">
        If something on either site regresses, or a test I haven't run yet turns something up, it
        gets a line in this log and the old entries stay. A page like this is only worth anything
        if it can report bad news.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>
</Layout>
